{#- Parts every integration guide shares, written once so forty pages say
    the same thing the same way. Each macro returns markdown, not HTML: the
    page is rendered by Nunjucks first and markdown-it after, so a fence
    inside a macro is a fence on the page. Nothing here is indented, since
    indentation inside a macro is indentation in the markdown.

    whyTarget(tool): why a pipeline that lowers CSS needs telling about
    Cirth's browsers, in one paragraph.

    checkIt(lowers, fix): the last step of every guide. A forced-dark subtree is
    the one thing a pipeline that rewrites light-dark() breaks, and the one
    thing a reader can see without a developer tool.

    nextSteps(where): where the reader's own overrides go. -#}

{%- macro whyTarget(tool = "Vite") -%}
{{ tool }} compiles CSS for older browsers than Cirth supports, and on the
way rewrites its `light-dark()` colours. The page still follows the system
between light and dark, but an element with `data-theme` that forces the
other scheme inside it stops changing colour. Tell {{ tool }} Cirth's
browser floor:
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro checkIt(lowers = false, fix = "check the CSS target above") -%}
Put a forced-dark subtree on any page:

```html
<section data-theme="dark">
  <article>
    <p>Dark, whatever the system scheme is.</p>
  </article>
</section>
```

Build for production and open the page. The article is dark on a light
page and stays dark on a dark one.{% if lowers %} If it follows the page
instead, the build rewrote Cirth's colours: {{ fix }}.{% endif %}
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro nextSteps(where = "in a stylesheet loaded after Cirth's") -%}
[Customization](/customization) covers the `--cirth-*` properties; set
yours {{ where }}. To use another build (classless, scoped, print) see
[What the package contains](/compatibility#what-the-package-contains).
{%- endmacro -%}
